[{"input":"{data-type=\"instructions\"}\n# Instructions\n\n- Build a scientific book on using APS in physics and related fields, including electromagnetics, elasticity, general relativity, quantum mechanics, quantum field theory, and beyond. Assume readers have an advanced undergraduate engineering background.\n- Prefix notebook names with a numerical order, such as 141_contents.\n- Treat notebooks as chapters by default. End every chapter or appendix with a cell containing a definition list of all new terms introduced in that notebook. That cell must contain only the definition list.\n- Mark all important terms in chapters and appendices as index terms.\n- Declare references in 371_references, ordered by their first appearance across the notebooks. Cite references in the notebooks; do not include “Further reading” sections in chapters or appendices.\n- Give main-content tables the .inline or .wide class, as appropriate.\n","output":"","result":"{data-type=\"instructions\"}\n# Instructions\n\n- Build a scientific book on using APS in physics and related fields, including electromagnetics, elasticity, general relativity, quantum mechanics, quantum field theory, and beyond. Assume readers have an advanced undergraduate engineering background.\n- Prefix notebook names with a numerical order, such as 141_contents.\n- Treat notebooks as chapters by default. End every chapter or appendix with a cell containing a definition list of all new terms introduced in that notebook. That cell must contain only the definition list.\n- Mark all important terms in chapters and appendices as index terms.\n- Declare references in 371_references, ordered by their first appearance across the notebooks. Cite references in the notebooks; do not include “Further reading” sections in chapters or appendices.\n- Give main-content tables the .inline or .wide class, as appropriate.\n","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"instructions\" id=\"nb-Physics_000_instructions-c6552-instructions\">\n<h1>Instructions</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Build a scientific book on using APS in physics and related fields, including electromagnetics, elasticity, general relativity, quantum mechanics, quantum field theory, and beyond. Assume readers have an advanced undergraduate engineering background.</li>\n<li>Prefix notebook names with a numerical order, such as 141_contents.</li>\n<li>Treat notebooks as chapters by default. End every chapter or appendix with a cell containing a definition list of all new terms introduced in that notebook. That cell must contain only the definition list.</li>\n<li>Mark all important terms in chapters and appendices as index terms.</li>\n<li>Declare references in 371_references, ordered by their first appearance across the notebooks. Cite references in the notebooks; do not include “Further reading” sections in chapters or appendices.</li>\n<li>Give main-content tables the .inline or .wide class, as appropriate.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null}]